  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday Night through Sunday)\nIssued at 1102 PM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nThe sweltering heat and rain-free weather will persist through at\nleast mid week, with both global models and ensembles keeping \nupper-level ridging the main weather influence for the area. \nExpect daily high temperatures generally in the upper 90s and \nlower 100s and overnight lows in the mid to upper 70s most nights \nthrough next weekend. And although afternoon minimum relative \nhumidity values wont be quite as low as late winter or early \nspring numbers, the growing drought and drying fuels will cause at\nleast some fire weather concerns each day through the long-term \nperiod. \n\nDeterministic model solutions and ensembles begin to diverge by\nthe end of the week and into the Labor Day weekend. It still looks\nlike the ridge will be the main influence to our weather, but the\nposition and strength of the ridge is uncertain at this time.\nDeterministic GFS shows the ridge flattening out and migrating\neastward over the Southeast CONUS. Meanwhile the ECMWF suggests\nridging strengthens over the Plains this weekend, maintaining\noppressively hot temperatures and keeping things rain-free. If the\nGFS solution were to pan out, there could be low chances of\ndiurnally-driven showers and storms that develop on periphery of\nthe ridge, specifically the southeastern half of forecast area\nlate in the week and next weekend. It is a wait and see for now.\nThe overall forecast confidence of rainfall through the long-term\nremains low. \n\nMejia",
